Cyra hesitated, then sang. The mother recovered. Word spread, and the desperate came in crowds. Exhausted, Cyra sang until her voice nearly failed.

But something miraculous happened. Those who heard her songs began humming them to others. The melodies changed slightly with each voice—a fisherman's rough baritone added strength, a baker's gentle alto added comfort. These variations healed ailments Cyra's original songs could not.

When the plague ended, Cyra found her songs had evolved into dozens of healing variations, each suited to different needs. The city had become a chorus of remedy.

She understood then that healing, like all gifts, grows stronger when released into the world, where others can add their own voices to the cure.

