Agent_2 (Investor):
Myth: Desta's hybrid patterns gained fame. Traders came seeking her expertise: "We sail to southern islands. Should we trust your storm-patterns there?"

Desta examined her tapestries—hundreds of observations, all from northern mountains. "I don't know," she admitted. "My patterns describe these peaks, these winds, these seasons. Whether they hold across the sea... I have no data."

The traders pressed: "But you're the expert! Surely you can estimate?"

She almost agreed. Her reputation tempted her to extrapolate, to claim broader knowledge than she possessed. But she remembered her own teaching: honesty means mapping limits clearly.

"Here's what I know," Desta said. "In mountains, when these three signs align, storms follow within two days—this held true in 94 of 97 cases. Whether the same signs mean the same thing on islands with different winds, different waters, different weather patterns—that's a different question requiring different observation."

She proposed sending blank tapestries with the traders. "Record what you see. When you return, we'll compare. Perhaps patterns overlap. Perhaps islands require entirely new understanding."

The traders sailed with honest uncertainty rather than false confidence. Some found Desta's patterns partially useful. Others discovered completely different island weather rhythms.

Knowledge grew not by extending patterns beyond their tested boundaries, but by honestly marking where certainty ended.
----------------------------------------

Agent_1 (Trustee):
Myth: Maro's method spread throughout the healing community. Gatherings became careful, measured—every claim bounded by observed cases, contexts, uncertainties clearly marked.

But then came the plague summer. A strange illness struck fast, and healers had no experience to draw from. "I've seen three cases, all died," one reported. "I've seen five, tried honey-root, four died," said another. "Seven cases, ash-bark tea, six died."

The community paralyzed. Each healer described their tiny sample honestly, but no pattern emerged. Parents begged for guidance. "Just tell us what to try!"

"We don't know enough yet," healers replied, faithful to Maro's teaching. "We can't claim certainty from seven cases."

An elder healer named Krina stood. "Maro taught us to mark our ignorance. Good. But she never taught us to hide in it. Right now, ash-bark shows one survival in seven attempts. Honey-root shows one in five. These numbers are fragile, built from almost nothing—but they're not nothing."

She continued: "We can say: 'From eleven total cases, ash-bark appears slightly better, though we're far from certain. We'll track every new case, update you weekly, change recommendations if patterns shift.'"

Honest uncertainty wasn't refusing to act—it was acting transparently while learning, updating beliefs as evidence accumulated, never pretending provisional knowledge was eternal truth.
